From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: playportdev <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 15:37:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: place a request the furniture ceiling clamped above
 the ceiling

Death's Door (Unity, D3D11) quit 14 s in on every launch with DXMT's
MTLD3DError: its d3d11.dll created a thread, and the thread's 1 MB emulator
stack could not be placed. The furniture ceiling clamps every request without
a limit_high to [floor, 0x73ffff0000), a 16 GB window of which 8 GB is the
ml433 holdback and 0.5 GB the JIT pool's RW alias. The game's ~7.6 GB of
views (FEX ~4 GB, UnityPlayer.dll 2.2 GB, d3d11.dll 0.6 GB) filled the rest.

Requests with a 4 GB floor (limit_4g: every x64 thread's CHPE emulator stack
and a WoW64 thread's 64-bit stack) are not ceiling_relaxable, since
address_space_start is 0x10000 after set_large_address_space, so they failed
with 30 GB free above the ceiling. The relaxable ones escaped through a kernel
pick, which put them in iOS's own low VA [4 GB, 64 GB), where the host's
allocators have their last free holes.

Now a request the ceiling clamped first scans above the ceiling, from
max(limit_low, ceiling) to the user limit, bottom-up, before the kernel pick
or a refusal. The caller's own limits still hold: limit_low stays the floor,
and a caller with a limit_high is never clamped. [va-ceiling] logs the first
16 placements and every failure.

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-06-guest-va-exhaustion.md
Offered-upstream: no
---
 build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c | 34 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c b/build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c
index e1da28e..189749e 100644
--- a/build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c
+++ b/build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c
@@ -12002,10 +12002,12 @@ static NTSTATUS map_view( struct file_view **view_ret, void *base, size_t size,
          * top-of-space tenant from ml106) stay below 464G-64K; only the PA
          * 16GB/32GB pool reserves may use the slots above. */
         int ceiling_relaxable = 0;
+        int ceiling_applied = 0;
         if (ios_furniture_ceiling && !limit_high && size < 0x400000000ULL &&
             (void *)ios_furniture_ceiling < end)
         {
             end = (void *)ios_furniture_ceiling;
+            ceiling_applied = 1;
             /* Relaxable when the only *effective* constraint is ours. ml119
              * BUG: the first cut used !limit_low, so a caller passing a small
              * non-zero limit_low (<= address_space_start, i.e. not actually
@@ -12108,7 +12110,7 @@ static NTSTATUS map_view( struct file_view **view_ret, void *base, size_t size,
                          ios_scan_stop_name( ios_scan_stop ),
                          ios_scan_fail_addr, ios_scan_fail_errno,
                          ios_scan_fail_errno ? strerror( ios_scan_fail_errno ) : "-", what,
-                         ptr ? "" : (ceiling_relaxable ? "  --> relaxing ceiling, retrying unclamped"
+                         ptr ? "" : (ceiling_applied ? "  --> retrying above the ceiling"
                                                        : "  <-- STATUS_NO_MEMORY (callers see a NULL alloc)") );
                 }
             }
@@ -12117,6 +12119,36 @@ static NTSTATUS map_view( struct file_view **view_ret, void *base, size_t size,
                 TRACE( "got mem with map_free_area %p-%p\n", ptr, (char *)ptr + size );
                 goto done;
             }
+            /* Playport: the ceiling is ours, so a request it clamped tries the space
+             * above it before anything else, for every caller whose own limits allow
+             * it: limit_low (if any) stays the floor, and a caller with a limit_high
+             * was never clamped. Death's Door filled the clamped window (8 GB of it
+             * is the ml433 holdback) 14 s in. The 4 GB-floored requests, every x64
+             * thread's emulator stack among them, are not ceiling_relaxable, so they
+             * failed with 30 GB free above the ceiling, and DXMT threw when its
+             * thread could not start. The relaxable ones escaped through a kernel
+             * pick, which put them in iOS's own low VA ([4 GB, 64 GB)), the last
+             * free holes the host's allocators have. Above the ceiling is
+             * [0x73ffff0000, 0x7c00000000) before the FEX band. */
+            if (ceiling_applied)
+            {
+                void *above_lo = (void *)ios_furniture_ceiling;
+                void *above_hi = min( user_space_limit, host_addr_space_limit );
+                unsigned int above_tries = ios_va_scan_tries;
+
+                if (limit_low && (void *)limit_low > above_lo) above_lo = (void *)limit_low;
+                if ((char *)above_hi > (char *)above_lo &&
+                    (size_t)((char *)above_hi - (char *)above_lo) >= view_size)
+                    ptr = map_free_area( above_lo, above_hi, host_size, 0, unix_prot, align_mask );
+                {
+                    static unsigned above_lines;
+                    if (!ptr || above_lines++ < 16)
+                        dprintf( 2, "[va-ceiling] size=%p limit_low=%p above the ceiling: %s %p (tries=%u)\n",
+                                 (void *)size, (void *)limit_low, ptr ? "placed at" : "FAILED, none in",
+                                 ptr ? ptr : above_lo, ios_va_scan_tries - above_tries );
+                }
+                if (ptr) goto done;
+            }
             /* ml117: THE CEILING IS ADVISORY, NEVER FATAL. It exists only to
              * bias Wine's furniture low so the top 16GB-aligned slots stay free
              * for PartitionAlloc; failing an allocation for it is strictly worse
